From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LLCOOLT <114613597+LLCOOLT@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] gx: the texture content cache keeps the frame's working set;
 texture object IDs wrap

The content cache is an LRU of 128 MB, trimmed as each new texture goes in.
A frame that draws more than that (textures upscaled 4x in a room full of
sprites: Rogueport's plaza with four Paper Mario 64 bosses) evicted, from the
back, the textures it had drawn the frame before and was still to draw this
one, made each again (an upload, upscaled again) and evicted the next: 500
uploads and 24 MB a frame, as many upload buffers of their own, 1 fps and a
phone out of memory. LruCacheLimits.workingSetFrames: entries used within that
many frames (this one counted) are passed over by a budget trim, so the cache
grows past its budget for the frame's own textures and is trimmed back at a
frame's end once they are left alone. A set budget (set_budget: tests, a
flush) is still met at once. The content cache keeps two frames' (this one and
the last); the other caches are unchanged (0: plain LRU). The cache's frame
count now advances on a budget-only cache too.

The texture and TLUT object IDs are a u32 counter, one per GXInitTexObj (most
draws make one each frame): its end was a FATAL, a day or two of play, hours
in a busy room on a phone. It now starts again at 1 and drops what was cached
per object; an ID given again while an older object with it is still drawn is
told apart by its source (data, size and format; a TLUT's data, entries and
format), checked on each object-cache hit.
---
diff --git a/lib/dolphin/gx/GXTexture.cpp b/lib/dolphin/gx/GXTexture.cpp
index 080cf0d..0bad64e 100644
--- a/lib/dolphin/gx/GXTexture.cpp
+++ b/lib/dolphin/gx/GXTexture.cpp
@@ -21,16 +21,22 @@ constexpr u8 GX2HWFiltConv[6] = {0x00, 0x04, 0x01, 0x05, 0x02, 0x06};
 u32 sNextTexObjId = 1;
 u32 sNextTlutObjId = 1;
 
+// A game makes a texture object for most draws (GXInitTexObj on the stack, each frame): 2^32 of them is a day or
+// two of play, hours in a busy room. Past the last the IDs start again at 1 and what was cached per object is
+// dropped; an ID given again while an object that had it is still drawn is told apart by its source (texture.cpp's
+// object caches check the data, size and format, not the ID alone).
 u32 next_tex_obj_id() {
   if (sNextTexObjId == 0) {
-    FATAL("texObj ID overflow");
+    sNextTexObjId = 1;
+    aurora::gx::clear_static_texture_cache();
   }
   return sNextTexObjId++;
 }
 
 u32 next_tlut_obj_id() {
   if (sNextTlutObjId == 0) {
-    FATAL("tlutObj ID overflow");
+    sNextTlutObjId = 1;
+    aurora::gx::clear_static_texture_cache();
   }
   return sNextTlutObjId++;
 }
diff --git a/lib/gfx/lru_cache.hpp b/lib/gfx/lru_cache.hpp
index 714ee12..42e26de 100644
--- a/lib/gfx/lru_cache.hpp
+++ b/lib/gfx/lru_cache.hpp
@@ -16,6 +16,11 @@ struct LruCacheLimits {
   uint64_t bytes;
   std::optional<uint64_t> idleFrames = std::nullopt;
   uint64_t sweepFrames = 1;
+  // Entries used in this frame or the ones before it (this many frames, counting this one) are the working set:
+  // a budget trim passes over them (the cache grows past its budget for them, and is trimmed back once they are
+  // left alone), as evicting one only makes it again (an upload, its texture upscaled again) the next time the
